Media player plugin for DC++ Experimental
========================================

Version 0.5 targets the Windows API 8 plugin interface used by
DCPlusPlus-No-MDI 0.8842. The plugin stays in its own repository; no DC++
source changes are required. The bundled legacy SDK is ABI-compatible with
the interfaces used here; the integration test compiles against the actual
DC++ checkout's dcpp/plugins/PluginDefs.h to check the DLL boundary.

Build, test and package (PowerShell, MinGW-w64 x64)
------------------------------------------------
Put CMake 3.25+, g++, windres and mingw32-make on PATH, then run here:

    cmake -S . -B build/mingw-release -G "MinGW Makefiles" -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DDCPP_SOURCE_DIR=../DCPlusPlus-No-MDI
    cmake --build build/mingw-release --parallel
    ctest --test-dir build/mingw-release --output-on-failure --timeout 30
    cmake --build build/mingw-release --target package_dcext

Outputs:
    build/mingw-release/MediaPlayer-x64.dll
    build/mingw-release/MediaPlayer-0.5-x64-Release.dcext

The package includes info.xml, the DLL, and MediaPlayer.ico. It contains
only the selected architecture, with no missing PDB references. The MinGW
runtime is linked statically so the installed plugin needs no MinGW DLLs.

For another checkout, pass its path as DCPP_SOURCE_DIR. To build without
the integration test or a DC++ source checkout, use -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF.
CMake also supports MSVC and 32-bit toolchains, but the validated build is
MinGW-w64 GCC 13.2.0 x64. The DLL architecture must match DC++.
The old projects/ files are retained; CMake is the maintained build path.

Install and use
---------------
Open DC++'s plugin settings (the Plugins toolbar button), choose Add, and
select the .dcext above. Accept the package installation dialog. If an older
copy of this plugin is installed, disable it and restart DC++ before updating.
The existing plugin GUID and saved formats are retained.

Use the "Configure media broadcasts" plugin command to edit the Winamp
format. An empty format restores the default. Supported commands in hub
chat and private messages:

    /w, /wa, /winamp     Broadcast Winamp's current title and playback info.
    /mpc                Broadcast the Media Player Classic window title.
    /help plugins       Display this plugin's command help.

Winamp must expose the "Winamp v1.x" window class; MPC must expose
"MediaPlayerClassicW". MPC variants with other window classes and other
players are not supported. The format can start with "/me " for an action
message. Winamp placeholders are listed in the configuration dialog's Help.
MPC uses its stored MPCFormat setting, defaulting to "NP > %[title]".
Missing-player errors stay local, with a notification in private chat.

Compatibility changes
---------------------
- Repeatable Unicode x64 DLL and .dcext build without an MFC dependency.
- Fully initialized metadata and failed-load/duplicate-load cleanup.
- Configuration owned by the DC++ window, reusable while open, and safe
  destruction on close, disable and uninstall.
- Bounded format/title buffers and support for short formats such as %[title].
- Preserve Unicode/plain Winamp titles when playlist decorations are absent.
- Honor the MPC format and handle failed PM commands locally.
- Initialize missing settings while preserving existing formats.

Validation
----------
The DLL integration test builds against DCPlusPlus-No-MDI's host header
and provides local substitutes for the used host interfaces and player
windows. It checks the exported entry point, metadata, rejected loads,
load/install/unload/reload, hook/menu cleanup, hub/PM commands, /me, absent
players, Unicode and long titles, format persistence, dialog ownership,
and dialog closure during unload. No network traffic is sent. Close Winamp
and MPC before running it; the test refuses to use live player windows.

Validated against DC++ Experimental 0.8842 (checkout 270b0a5), Windows x64,
MinGW-w64 GCC 13.2.0. Real Winamp/MPC playback in a running DC++ session has
not been manually tested. The package is built but is not automatically
installed into a user's DC++ profile.

Historical SDK and package-format documentation is under Doc/.
